 ],
 "literal": "Pond's water lily's beautiful-colored plump bud / the bee opens its mouth [at], of the cool-shore town's chief with, / if [we] sit, on both sides [we] are; if [we] lie down, / like [the string] fitted to the finger inside the bow, he / if [we] embrace [his] good chest, on one side [we] are.",
 "translation": "With the chief of the cool-shored town, where bees open the plump, beautifully colored buds of the pond's water lilies: if we sit, we are on two separate sides; but if we lie down and embrace his beautiful chest, we are on one side, like the bow-string fitted to the finger inside the bow.",
 "modern": "குளத்து ஆம்பலின் அழகிய நிறமுடைய பருத்த மொட்டுகளை வண்டுகள் வாய் திறந்து தேன் உண்ணும் குளிர்ந்த துறையை உடைய ஊரனோடு, நாம் அமர்ந்திருந்தால் இரு பக்கங்களிலும் தனித்தனியாக இருக்கிறோம்; ஆனால் படுத்துக்கொண்டு அவனுடைய நல்ல மார்பைத் தழுவினால், வில்லின் உள்ளே விரலோடு பொருந்திய நாணினைப் போல, நாம் ஒரே பக்கமாக ஒன்றிவிடுகிறோம்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the courtesan (parattai) about her intimate union with the hero. The setting belongs to the Marutam (agricultural) landscape, where ponds, water lilies, and bees represent fertility and sensory pleasures. The courtesan describes how, when she and the hero merely sit together, they remain separate individuals on two sides. However, when they lie down and embrace, they merge into a single entity. She compares this physical and emotional union to an archer's finger fitted perfectly to the bow-string inside the bow, where the string and the finger align as one. The key words are 'இரு மருங்கினமே' (on two sides) and 'ஒரு மருங்கினமே' (on one side).",
